  "image_name": "cbd4ffab54ed4a385d4a2aacc9986a0a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how modifying catalyst surfaces can tune hydrogenation reactions. Across the top, the message is that changing a metal electronically or spatially—by adding dopant atoms, creating isolated sites, or introducing adsorbed species—alters how surface hydrogen behaves and can suppress direct H–H recombination or change hydrogen availability.\n\nThe rest of the figure links these surface changes to selectivity. For semihydrogenation, modified surfaces such as P-modified Pd or Cu clusters favor stopping at partially hydrogenated products, such as converting alkynes to alkenes, rather than continuing to full saturation. In contrast, unmodified Pd and low-coordinated Cu are shown as promoting full hydrogenation, driving alkynes or nitriles through intermediates to alkanes or amines.\n\nA final section highlights functional-group compatibility. On conventional surfaces, a substrate bearing another functional group can undergo unwanted co-hydrogenation of that group. More isolated or tailored active sites appear to retain the functional group while reducing only the targeted unsaturated bond, improving chemoselectivity. Overall, the figure presents catalyst design as a way to control hydrogen activation, intermediate binding, and site isolation in order to steer reactions toward selective partial hydrogenation while preserving sensitive groups.",
